[Music]  despite the passage of 26 years and 11  main entries there has never been a  better time to try Mortal Kombat with a  potent mixture of great tutorials clever  character design and innovative combat  mechanics nether realms newest fighting  game introduces newcomers without  sacrificing depth  it offers separate starting points for  players who prefer story or competition  or customization  simply put Mortal Kombat 11 is a  fighting game for everyone and that's  awesome another realms previous game  2007 teens injustice 2 achieved a  similar effect albeit through different  means  it used DC's brand awareness to bring in  fans of Batman Superman associated  friends and foes before unfurling a huge  loot grind that encouraged  experimentation across several modes it  struck a careful balance between  challenge and accessibility I enjoyed it  so much I made a video about it with  Mortal Kombat 11 it feels as if  NetherRealm took that balance and ran  with it  it displays the hardcore fighting  aspects it's mastered over almost three  decades but conversely it's also the  studio's most accessible title yet I  myself have never been a hardcore  fighting game fan I complete story mode  spend a few nights playing with friends  and usually move on  for me the genre has been a series of  short-term relationships I loved each  game in its own way but I couldn't  commit yet Mortal Kombat 11 hooked me in  a way I did not expect I actively want  to get better this all began as it often  does with the narrative this dumb  fantastical time-bending fire-breathing  spine ripping utterly ridiculous tale of  love loss and how much Kano [ __ ]  sucks  it introduced me to some of my favorite  characters and encouraged me to dig  deeper  it was the pickaxe the Mortal Kombat's  shimmering gold mine shortly after  defeating chronica and her minions  I struck the next layer nether realms  fantastic tutorials they begin with  moves as basic as a front punch or a  back kick before transitioning into -  cancels and flawless blocks before long  I found myself studying frame data and  strategy contrary to most fighting game  tutorials and which I usually give up  once I hit a skill I can't fathom using  in an actual match MK 11 shows me why I  should consider mastering that skill  what's crucial here is that with a  single button press you can practice the  timing of these advanced maneuvers while  MK 11 si I demonstrates them I was this  close to giving up on get up attacks  before I push my touchpad practice the  button timing and soon understood how  useful this skill could be I'm still not  great at pulling them off but I  understand them and this constant  learning gives me a deeper appreciation  for what I already considered a very fun  game I was so engrossed in learning  Mortal Kombat 11s Universal mechanics  that it took me a while to notice its  inclusion of character specific  tutorials these go beyond the standard  practice sessions I normally use to  learn a character they actively explain  a fighter's best functions along with  his or her strengths and weaknesses some  are obvious like Aaron blacks role as as  owner others take a bit more time to  discover such as noob saibot sibility to  use a crushing blow against an enemy  that's blocking high each character is  as complicated as they are flashy and  the tutorials are an excellent way to  explore their layers this brings me to  Mortal Kombat's characters themselves  and I'm actively restraining myself from  going off for another 30 minutes I'm  obsessed with the intricacies of  character design in genres ranging from  first-person shooters to tactical  strategy titles how different fighters  or soldiers factor into the overall flow  of a game how they reinforce its ideas  or transform its systems and Mortal  Kombat Elevens characters are colorful  proof of nether realms design acumen in  a recent video about warframe I explored  the concept of lenticular design it's a  phrase coined by Magic the Gathering  lead designer mark rosewater used to  describe systems that are simultaneously  simple to beginners and complex to  veterans and like warframe MK 11 makes  great use of this practice take scorpion  one of the franchise's most iconic  characters at first blush he's an agile  ninja equipped with katanas and a  grappling hook right away these weapons  make it clear that he can excel at  close-range or from long  distances as you learn him you'll likely  benefit from a combination of both  however once you dig into his special  moves you'll discover his more  supernatural powers namely  flame attacks and teleportation  experience scorpion players will know  how to throw off their enemy by using  his whole moveset sure you can win a  fight just by punching kicking and  swinging your sword but you'll win more  constantly by switching up your  offensive strategy this balance between  obvious abilities and more complicated  and often more useful powers is what  makes scorpion a lenticular character  and it's not just MK Elevens characters  that demonstrate this dichotomy so - -  the new fatal blows these maneuvers give  you a fighting chance when getting your  ass kicked by another player once your  health drops below 30% you gain access  to this powerful attack that melts away  more health than any other one attack in  your fighters arsenal it doesn't  completely level the playing field each  time but it does keep matches  interesting more often than not you also  only get to use one successful fatal  blow per match so when you use it is an  important decision in its own right I've  seen fatal blows compared to Mario karts  infamous blue shells but that's  hyperbolic to say the least  unlike Mario karts a rubber band item  fatal blows don't completely negate the  skill of the best player they merely  bolster that of the weaker or unluckier  one there require thought and strategy  to use correctly  we always talk about character design  and special moves it's time I addressed  Mk 11s loot system which has recently  drawn a lot of criticism and in this  regard nether realm both reinforces and  completely undermines the entire point  of this video so thanks a ton nether  realm on the bright side the fighting  games loot system is yet another way it  brings players into new modes  encouraging them to try the towers of  time or classic challenges and rewarding  them with a continual supply of  cosmetics ability augments and gory  cinematic moves on the not so bright  side if you want them all it takes a  very long time or a lot of money to  unlock everyone this runs completely  against the intelligent systems  throughout the rest of the game ones  that are so good about welcoming new  players and rewarding dedicated ones now  another realm has promised it's working  on fixing the loot system to better  respect players time I hope it does at  its best Mortal Kombat's loot amplifies  the joys of exploring its roster by  unlocking a new cosmetic item for gear  us or a new fatality for cabal the game  entices me to try characters I otherwise  might not as it stands now though MK 11  suite of unlockables feels borderline  predatory and that's a huge bummer  regardless of whether nether realm  actually does improve that aspect of its  newest release I can't help but love  everything else it has to offer for the  first time I'm not only excited to keep  learning a fighting game but almost  every layer of the game is helping me to  do so there are brick walls here and  there when a boss repeatedly destroys me  or when my friend Dan won't stop mashing  his controller and somehow winning no  matter what tactics I use against him  and how much I let my anger feel my  every waking hour getting better so I  can beat him and finally laugh in his  face but this is that rare kind of game  that makes breaking through those walls  fun in almost every layer of its design  Mortal Kombat 11 is a fighting game for  everyone and it's all the better because  of it  you  [Music]

